#!
.fullspan
  .center
    ![Logo](../image/new_logo.png)

    ---
    #! Groundwater -- Principles
    ###! EMSC3025/6025
    ---

    ###! _Dr. Sia Ghelichkhan_

# Objectives

.row
  .container width:{100%}
    By the end of this module, you will be able to:

    - **Separate storage from transmission**: explain why porosity tells you how much water a rock holds and nothing about how fast it gives it up
    - **Use hydraulic head**: explain why head, and not pressure, is the quantity that decides which way water flows, and calculate it from a field measurement
    - **Apply Darcy's Law**: calculate specific discharge and pore velocity, and say where the law stops working
    - **Explain where the law comes from**: derive the dependence of conductivity on the square of the grain size, and use it to read the empirical formulas
    - **Handle layered ground**: compute equivalent horizontal and vertical conductivity, and explain why layering alone can make a rock a million times easier to cross one way than the other
    - **Read field data**: interpret piezometer nests, potentiometric maps and water-level hydrographs
    - **Weigh a basin from orbit**: explain how GRACE measures total water storage and how groundwater is separated from it


# Two questions, and they are independent

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    Everything in this lecture answers one of two questions about a piece of ground.

    .box title:{1. How much water is in there?}
      Answered by **porosity**. A property of the void space.

    .box title:{2. How fast will it come out?} background:{.lightblue}
      Answered by **hydraulic conductivity**. A property of how the voids connect.

    The mistake to avoid for the next twelve weeks: assuming that the answer to
    one tells you the answer to the other. It does not. The material with the
    highest porosity in this lecture is also the material with the lowest
    conductivity.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Wetland](../image/internet_wetland_info.png "From wetlands to aquifers: this module is about what happens between the two.")


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Porosity: how much water the ground holds


# Porosity of a Soil or Rock

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - **Porosity** is the fraction of a volume of rock or soil that is void space.
    - **Total porosity** $ n_T $ is defined as:

      $ n_T = \frac{V_v}{V_T} = \frac{V_T - V_s}{V_T} $

      where $ V_v $ is the volume of voids, $ V_s $ the volume of solids and $ V_T $ the total volume.
    - Since $ V_v < V_T $, porosity is always a fraction below 1. It is often quoted as a percentage.
    - In the laboratory it is easier to weigh than to measure void volume, so the same quantity is obtained from densities:

      $ n_T = 1 - \frac{\rho_b}{\rho_s} $

      with $ \rho_b $ the **bulk density** of the dry sample and $ \rho_s $ the **particle density** of the solids.
    - For most silicate minerals $ \rho_s \approx 2650 $ kg/m³, so a dry bulk density of 1600 kg/m³ gives $ n_T = 0.40 $.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Porosity Schematic](../image/internet_porosity.jpg "Void space is what holds the water. How the voids connect is a separate question.")


# What sets porosity: packing

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - For grains of **one size**, porosity is set by how they are stacked.
    - Take identical spheres. The loosest stable stacking is cubic, each sphere sitting directly on the one below:

      $ n_T = 1 - \frac{\pi}{6} = 0.476 $

    - The tightest is rhombohedral, each sphere resting in the hollow between three others:

      $ n_T = 1 - \frac{\pi}{3\sqrt{2}} = 0.260 $

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Both numbers are **independent of the size of the spheres**. Void volume and total volume both scale as the cube of the radius, so the ratio does not change. Marbles and beach balls have the same porosity.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Sphere packing and porosity](../image/franklin_fig_3-2.png "Sections of four contiguous spheres of equal size: in (a) the most compact arrangement and the lowest porosity; in (b) less compact; in panel c, least compact and the highest porosity.")

    Compaction moves a real sediment from the loose end of that range towards the tight end. It does not change the grain size.


# What sets porosity: sorting and shape

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - **Sorting** is the spread of grain sizes in a sample.
      - **Well sorted** (one size) → porosity near the sphere-packing values above.
      - **Poorly sorted** (mixed sizes) → small grains sit in the voids between large ones, and porosity falls.
      - Glacial till is the standard example: sand, silt and clay together, mean porosity 23.5%, the lowest of any unlithified material in the table.
    - **Grain shape** moves the result in either direction. Angular and platy grains bridge against each other and hold open voids that spheres would close; they can also interlock and close voids that spheres would hold open. Shape alone does not fix the sign.
    - **Clays** reach 40 to 55% because their platy particles stack edge to face in an open framework held by surface charge, and not because their grains are small.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{The rule that catches people} background:{.lightred}
      "Smaller grains, more porosity" is true as a description of the table of measured values, and it is not a mechanism. Within one well-sorted material the grain size does not enter at all. Clay is porous because of how the particles are shaped and charged.

    Grain size returns with full force in the next section, where it controls **conductivity** as the square of the diameter.


# Primary and Secondary Porosity

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    - **Primary porosity**: the voids that formed with the rock.
      - Pores between grains in a sediment or sandstone
      - Vesicles, lava tubes and cooling fractures in basalt
    - **Secondary porosity**: voids created afterwards.
      - Joints and faults in igneous, metamorphic and consolidated sedimentary rock
      - Solution-enlarged openings in carbonates and other soluble rock
    - The distinction matters because the two behave differently: primary porosity is distributed through the rock, secondary porosity is concentrated on surfaces that may or may not connect to each other.

  .container width:{55%}
    !(100%)[Types of openings](../image/franklin_fig_3-1.png "Types of openings in water-bearing rocks. (a) is a few millimetres wide and shows primary porosity in unlithified sand. panels b and c are metres to tens of metres wide: fracturing in granite, and caverns in limestone produced by solution along fractures.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{2px}
      Knowing that a unit is "limestone" tells you almost nothing about its porosity. Its history does.


# Porosity in natural materials

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    | Material          | Min    | Mean  | Max  | SD   |
    |-------------------|--------|-------|------|------|
    | **Unlithified deposits** |        |       |      |      |
    | Sand              | 28.1   | 38.9  | 44.4 | 4.9  |
    | Dune              | c      | 42.1  | c    | 8.3  |
    | Silt              | 31.5   | 45.2  | 50.8 | 5.6  |
    | Clay              | 40.1   | 46.1  | 55   | 4.5  |
    | Glacial till      | 14.3   | 23.5  | 34   | c    |
    | Peat              | c      | 92    | c    | c    |

    Values in per cent. "c" marks too few measurements to quote. Sand and gravel in the field are usually quoted more narrowly, 25 to 35%, because the laboratory samples behind this table are loosely packed.

  .container width:{50%}
    | Material          | Min    | Mean  | Max  | SD   |
    |-------------------|--------|-------|------|------|
    | **Sedimentary rocks**    |        |       |      |      |
    | Sandstone         | 4      | 20.4  | 28.6  | 8.6  |
    | Limestone         | 0.44   | 8.67  | 34.7 | 8.6  |
    | Dolostone         | 0.4    | 7.46  | 26   | c    |
    | Shale             | 0.75   | 10.8  | 27.2 | 8.3  |
    | **Igneous rocks**        |        |       |      |      |
    | Granite           | <0.001 |       | 0.01 |      |
    | Basalt            | <0.01  |       | 0.1  |      |

    Cementation is what separates sandstone from sand. Burial and diagenesis reduce porosity with age and depth. Unaltered igneous rock has effectively none, which is why the fracturing in the previous figure is the whole story for granite.


# Effective porosity

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - Not every void carries flow. **Effective porosity** $ n_e $ is the fraction of the volume made of voids that are connected along a path through the sample.
    - In a clean sand or gravel the pores are all connected, and $ n_e \approx n_T $.
    - In a fractured rock they are not. An unfractured shale holds water in poorly connected pores; the water that moves, moves in the fractures. There the **fracture porosity** $ n_f $ is the effective porosity.
    - Fracture apertures away from outcrop are tens to hundreds of micrometres. Fractures 100 µm wide spaced a metre apart give

      $ n_f = \frac{10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m}}{1\,\mathrm{m}} = 10^{-4} $

    - Typical fracture porosities are $ 10^{-4} $ to $ 10^{-3} $, against a total porosity of 0.10 for the same shale: a factor of a thousand.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(78%)[Fractured Coconino Sandstone](../image/franklin_fig_3-3.png "Fractured Coconino Sandstone, northeastern Arizona. The fracturing runs both vertically and horizontally, and varies from place to place in one unit.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      Schwartz & Zhang p. 45 gives $ n_f \approx 5\times10^{-2} $ for shale. That is a misprint: the book's own worked example uses $ 3\times10^{-3} $.


# Dual porosity

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    A fractured rock has **two** porosities at once, and they do different jobs.

    | | Matrix | Fractures |
    |---|---|---|
    | Porosity | 0.05 – 0.15 | $ 10^{-4} $ – $ 10^{-3} $ |
    | Holds the water | **yes** | no |
    | Carries the flow | no | **yes** |

    - Almost all of the water is in the matrix. Almost all of the movement is in the fractures.
    - A contaminant travelling in the fractures diffuses slowly into the matrix, and then leaks back out for decades after the fractures have been flushed. This is why fractured-rock contamination is so hard to clean up, and we return to it later in the course.
    - The same two-compartment picture describes karst, where the fractures have been enlarged by dissolution into conduits.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Caverns and fractures](../image/internet_porosity_primarysecondary.png "Primary porosity between grains; secondary porosity along fractures and solution openings.")


# The central result of this lecture

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Two wells, drilled to the same depth, in two materials with the **same porosity** of 0.30.

    | | Gravel | Silt |
    |---|---|---|
    | Porosity | 0.30 | 0.30 |
    | Water stored per m³ | 300 L | 300 L |
    | Hydraulic conductivity | $ 10^{2} $ m/day | $ 10^{-2} $ m/day |
    | Yield to a well | supplies a town | fills a bucket |

    The two columns hold identical quantities of water. One of them will give it to you.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{And the extreme case} background:{.lightred}
      **Clay** has the highest mean porosity of any material in the table on the previous slide, 46%. It also has the lowest hydraulic conductivity of any material in the figure two sections from now. Clay is nearly half water by volume and will not give you a drink.

    - Porosity sets **storage**.
    - Conductivity sets **transmission**.
    - An aquifer needs both. A unit with high porosity and low conductivity is a **confining bed**, and that is the subject of next week's lecture.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Occurrence: where the water sits


# Occurrence of Groundwater

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - In the shallow subsurface the pores hold **both water and air**: the **unsaturated zone**. Water content there rises with infiltration and falls as plants and evaporation take it back. This is the water plants live on, and the subject of the soil moisture lecture.
    - Deeper, the pores hold **only water**: the **saturated zone**, and the water in it is **groundwater**.
    - The **water table** separates the two. It is the surface on which water pressure equals atmospheric pressure.
    - Just above it, surface tension holds a saturated layer at below-atmospheric pressure, the **capillary fringe**. By the definition above it is not groundwater. It is millimetres thick in gravel and metres thick in clay.
    - Water that gets past the root zone and reaches the water table is **recharge**.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(62%)[Subsurface water](../image/franklin_fig_3-4.png "Precipitation infiltrates the soil zone and percolates through the unsaturated zone. What reaches the water table becomes groundwater.")


# Flow of Groundwater

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    - Recharge arrives at the water table and then moves **down and sideways** towards a discharge point: a river, a spring, a wetland, the sea.
    - Both parts of that motion are driven by **one** quantity, the gradient in hydraulic head. The next section defines it, and shows why it is not the pressure gradient.
    - Surface runoff and groundwater discharge end up in the same river. The difference is the time taken.
    - This is where the baseflow of the runoff lecture comes from. The reason a river still flows in October is that water which fell in some earlier year is still on its way.

  .container width:{55%}
    !(100%)[A groundwater flow system](../image/franklin_fig_3-5.png "Some precipitation becomes recharge; groundwater moves down and laterally and discharges into the nearby river.")

    .align{center}
      .container alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{5px}
        Surface runoff reaches the stream in hours. The groundwater beside it takes years.


# How long does it take?

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    Residence time is path length divided by the speed along the path, and the speed comes from the section after next:

    $ t = \frac{L}{v} = \frac{L\, n_e}{K\, i} $

    | System | $ L $ | $ v $ | $ t $ |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Hillslope to creek, sand | 300 m | 0.15 m/day | 5 years |
    | Small basin, sand and gravel | 20 km | 0.5 m/day | 100 years |
    | Great Artesian Basin | 1000 km | 1 m/year | $ 10^{5} $ – $ 10^{6} $ years |

    - The recharge that feeds a bore in western Queensland fell on the Great Dividing Range before the last glacial period.
    - This is the residence time from the water cycle lecture, computed for one flow path instead of a whole reservoir.

  .container width:{45%}
    .box title:{Why this matters for management} background:{.lightred}
      A store with a residence time of $ 10^{5} $ years is not refilled on any timescale a water plan can use. Pumping it is mining, and the question is how fast to mine, not whether it recovers.

    We return to this in the groundwater management lecture, and the same arithmetic appears in Assignment III.


# Groundwater flow in the lab
