#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Hydraulic head: the quantity that decides the direction


# Water does not flow from high pressure to low pressure

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Take a column of water standing still in a tube. Pressure at depth $ d $ below the surface is

    $ P = \rho_w g d $

    - The pressure at the bottom is larger than the pressure at the top. The pressure gradient points upward, so a pressure rule predicts flow upward.
    - Nothing moves. The column is in equilibrium.
    - The same is true below a water table: pressure increases downward everywhere, in ground where no water is moving at all.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      **Pressure alone cannot tell you the direction of flow.** The quantity that can is hydraulic head.

  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 380 230" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <defs><marker id="ar" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
        <text x="110" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">pressure</text>
        <rect x="70" y="30" width="80" height="170" fill="#add8e6" stroke="#333" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="78" y1="60"  x2="98"  y2="60"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
        <line x1="78" y1="115" x2="120" y2="115" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
        <line x1="78" y1="170" x2="145" y2="170" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
        <text x="64" y="64"  font-size="11" text-anchor="end">small</text>
        <text x="64" y="119" font-size="11" text-anchor="end">larger</text>
        <text x="64" y="174" font-size="11" text-anchor="end">largest</text>
        <text x="300" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">head</text>
        <rect x="260" y="30" width="80" height="170" fill="#add8e6" stroke="#333" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="250" y1="30" x2="352" y2="30" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3"/>
        <line x1="275" y1="60"  x2="275" y2="32" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <line x1="300" y1="115" x2="300" y2="32" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <line x1="325" y1="170" x2="325" y2="32" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <circle cx="275" cy="60"  r="3" fill="#b43c3c"/>
        <circle cx="300" cy="115" r="3" fill="#b43c3c"/>
        <circle cx="325" cy="170" r="3" fill="#b43c3c"/>
        <text x="300" y="222" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">every point: the same h</text>
      </svg>

    Pressure varies from top to bottom of the column. Head does not. Where head is constant, there is no flow, whatever the pressure is doing.


# Hydraulic head

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    The energy of a unit weight of water at a point has three parts, which is the Bernoulli equation written in units of length:

    $ h = z + \frac{P}{\rho_w g} + \frac{v^2}{2g} $

    - $ z $: **elevation head**, the height of the point above the datum, usually sea level. This is the gravitational part.
    - $ P/\rho_w g $: **pressure head**, the height of the water column the pressure would support.
    - $ v^2/2g $: **velocity head**, the kinetic part.

    Groundwater moves at about 1 m/day, which is $ 1.2 \times 10^{-5} $ m/s, so

    $ \frac{v^2}{2g} = \frac{(1.2\times10^{-5})^2}{19.6} \approx 7\times10^{-12}\ \mathrm{m} $

    against head differences of metres. The velocity head is dropped, and

    $ h = z + \frac{P}{\rho_w g} $

  .container width:{45%}
    !(90%)[Head components](../image/franklin_fig_3-9.png "Elevation head, pressure head and total head at a point in the flow field.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Going down the still column raises $ P/\rho_w g $ by exactly what it takes off $ z $. The sum does not change.


# Measuring head in the field

.row
  .container width:{60%}
    A **standpipe** open at the bottom, and the water level in it, give the head at the open end.

    - In theory (a): read the elevation of the water column against a scale. In the figure, 110 m above sea level.
    - In practice (b): the pipe has no scale on it, so head is assembled from two measurements.
      - Survey or high-precision GPS gives the **elevation of the top of casing**, $ EL_{TOC} $ = 160 m.
      - A weighted electric tape gives the **depth to water**, $ DTW $ = 50 m.

      $ h = EL_{TOC} - DTW = 160 - 50 = 110\ \mathrm{m} $

    - The head belongs to the **measurement point**, which is the middle of the screen at the bottom of the pipe, and not to the water level in the pipe and not to the water table.
    - The screen is a slotted section of casing. In low-conductivity ground it lets the water level in the pipe keep up with changes outside.

  .container width:{40%}
    !(80%)[Measuring head](../image/franklin_fig_3-8.png "(a) Head as the elevation of the water column, 110 m asl, at the red measurement point. (b) The same head assembled from the casing elevation and the depth to water.")


# One site, two answers

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 330 220" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect x="0" y="45" width="330" height="175" fill="#f2ead9"/>
        <line x1="0" y1="45" x2="330" y2="45" stroke="#6b5b3e" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="70" y="30" width="16" height="160" fill="#cfcfcf" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="70" y="150" width="16" height="40" fill="#e8c96a" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="200" y="30" width="12" height="80" fill="#cfcfcf" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="200" y="95" width="12" height="15" fill="#e8c96a" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="70" y1="140" x2="86" y2="140" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="4"/>
        <line x1="200" y1="62" x2="212" y2="62" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="4"/>
        <text x="60" y="26" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">water well</text>
        <text x="206" y="26" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">piezometer</text>
        <text x="96" y="143" font-size="11" fill="#1f6fb2">h = 73 m</text>
        <text x="222" y="65" font-size="11" fill="#1f6fb2">h = 84 m</text>
        <line x1="0" y1="72" x2="330" y2="78" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-dasharray="5,4" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="240" y="92" font-size="9" fill="#1f6fb2">water table 81.5 m</text>
        <line x1="150" y1="120" x2="150" y2="165" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#a2)"/>
        <text x="156" y="148" font-size="10" fill="#b43c3c">flow</text>
        <defs><marker id="a2" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
      </svg>

  .container width:{55%}
    Two pipes, eleven metres apart, screened at different depths. The shallow one reads **84 m**. The deep one reads **73 m**.

    - Neither is wrong. There is no single "groundwater level" at this site.
    - The 11 m difference over that depth is a **downward** hydraulic gradient. Water is moving from the shallow unit into the deep aquifer.
    - Head is a property of a **point**, so a head measurement is meaningless until you say where the screen is and which unit it is in.
    - This is why piezometers are installed in **nests**: three short screens at three depths at one location, to measure how head changes with depth.
    - A single long-screened well averages over everything its screen crosses, and in a site like this it tells you nothing about the vertical gradient.


# The three devices

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - **Water wells**: wide casing, long screen. Cheap, already drilled in their thousands, and the only practical basis for regional work. Suited to a well-defined aquifer where flow is horizontal, because then equipotential lines are vertical and the depth of the screen does not change the reading.
    - **Piezometers**: narrow casing, short screen, sealed above with bentonite. A head measurement for a point. Used for contamination, for dewatering, and in nests for vertical gradients.
    - **Water table observation wells**: long screen deliberately straddling the range that the water table moves through, so the water table always sits somewhere in the screen.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      The observation well is the odd one out: its measurement point is **not** the middle of the screen. It is the water table, and it moves up and down through the year. Head at any point on the water table equals the elevation of that point.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Three devices](../image/franklin_fig_3-17.png "A water well, a water table observation well and a nest of three piezometers at one site. Heads: 73 m in the aquifer, 81.5 m at the water table, and 84, 81 and 78 m in the piezometer nest. Dashed lines are equipotentials; red arrows are flow directions.")

    In the figure the nest reads 84, 81 and 78 m from shallow to deep: head falls with depth, so flow in the shallow unit is downward.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Darcy's law


# Darcy's experiment

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - **Henry Darcy**, civil engineer, built the water supply of Dijon in the 1850s and needed to size the sand filters.
    - His apparatus: a cylinder of known cross-section area $ A $ packed with sand, water forced through at a measured rate $ Q $, and two manometers a distance $ \Delta l $ apart whose water levels give $ h_1 $ and $ h_2 $.
    - He varied the flow rate, the head difference, the length of the column and the sand, and found a single relationship:

      $ \frac{Q}{A} = -K \frac{dh}{dl} $

    - $ K $, the **hydraulic conductivity**, absorbs everything about the sand. It is a measured constant, not a derived one — the derivation came a century later, and we do it two slides from now.
    - This is an **empirical** law in exactly the same sense as Ohm's law, and it has the same form: a flux proportional to the gradient of a potential.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Darcy's apparatus](../image/franklin_fig_3-7.png "Laboratory apparatus demonstrating Darcy's equation: flow Q through a column of length Δl, with head h₁ and h₂ read from two manometers.")


# Darcy's law, and its sign

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    $ q = -K \frac{dh}{dl} \qquad Q = qA = -KA \frac{dh}{dl} $

    - $ q = Q/A $ is the **specific discharge** or **Darcy velocity**: volume per unit time through unit area of the whole column face. Units of m/day.
    - The minus sign is the physics: $ dh/dl $ is the change in head **in the direction you are walking**, so water moves in the direction in which head decreases.
    - If instead you define the gradient as a positive head loss over a positive distance,

      $ i = \frac{h_1 - h_2}{\Delta l} \quad (h_1 > h_2) $

      then $ q = Ki $ and the flow runs from 1 towards 2. Both forms are in use. Fix one, then keep it.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      Writing $ i = (h_1-h_2)/\Delta l $ **and** $ q = -Ki $ in the same breath, as the textbook does at its equation 3.5, gives a discharge that runs uphill. Note that $ (h_1-h_2)/\Delta l = -dh/dl $.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(78%)[Head gradient in a column](../image/franklin_fig_3-6.png "A dipping aquifer with head measured at two points: h₁ = 145 m, h₂ = 125 m, Δl = 5000 m, so i = 0.004.")

    The gradient is **dimensionless**: four millimetres of head lost per metre travelled.


# Darcy velocity is not a velocity

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - $ q $ is a discharge divided by the **whole** face of the column, solids included. No water travels at $ q $.
    - Water moves only through the connected voids, which are a fraction $ n_e $ of that face. The **average linear velocity**, or pore velocity, is therefore

      $ v = \frac{q}{n_e} = -\frac{K}{n_e}\frac{dh}{dl} $

    - Since $ n_e < 1 $, always $ v > q $.
    - $ v $ is the speed to use for travel times and for contaminant arrival.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      $ v $ is a **mean**. Individual paths through wide and narrow pores run at very different speeds, and that spread is what spreads a contaminant plume out along its own flow path. We give it a name, **hydrodynamic dispersion**, later in the course.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Pore velocity](../image/internet_pore_velocity_schematic.jpg "Flow takes place only in the connected pore space, so the true speed exceeds the Darcy velocity.")

    .align{center}
      .container
        [gw-project.org](https://books.gw-project.org/hydrogeologic-properties-of-earth-materials-and-principles-of-groundwater-flow/chapter/darcys-law/)


# Worked example: the same aquifer, twice

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Take $ K = 1\times10^{-4} $ m/s and $ i = 0.004 $ from the previous slide, and ask how fast the water actually travels.

    **(a) Granular sand,** $ n_e \approx n_T = 0.30 $:

    $ v = \frac{Ki}{n_e} = \frac{10^{-4}\times 0.004}{0.30} = 1.33\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m/s} $

    $ v = 0.115\ \mathrm{m/day} $

    **(b) Fractured shale,** same $ K $, $ n_T = 0.10 $, $ n_f = 3\times10^{-3} $:

    $ v = \frac{10^{-4}\times 0.004}{3\times10^{-3}} = 1.33\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m/s} $

    $ v = 11.5\ \mathrm{m/day} $

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Read the result} background:{.lightred}
      Same conductivity. Same gradient. Same discharge $ q $ through the face of the rock. **One hundred times** the travel speed, entirely because the flow is squeezed into a hundredth of the area.

    - A spill above the fractured unit reaches a bore 1 km away in **three months**. Above the sand it takes **24 years**.
    - This is the practical reason effective porosity is worth measuring, and the reason the misprinted $ n_f = 0.05 $ matters: it would have given 0.7 m/day, and a reassuring wrong answer.

    Careful with the book here: its printed answer for (a), 0.152 m/day, does not follow from its own numbers.


# Where Darcy's law comes from

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    Darcy measured $ K $. We can derive its form by treating the pore space as a bundle of tubes.

    For laminar flow in a tube of diameter $ d $, Poiseuille's result gives the mean velocity driven by a pressure gradient:

    $ \bar{v} = \frac{d^2}{32\mu}\left(-\frac{dP}{dx}\right) $

    Write the driving term with head, $ P = \rho_w g (h-z) $, so that $ -dP/dx = -\rho_w g\, dh/dx $:

    $ \bar{v} = \frac{\rho_w g\, d^2}{32\mu}\left(-\frac{dh}{dx}\right) $

    The specific discharge is $ q = n_e \bar{v} $ divided by a tortuosity factor, and all the dimensionless bookkeeping collects into one constant $ c $:

    $ q = \underbrace{\frac{c\,\rho_w g\, d^2}{\mu}}_{K}\left(-\frac{dh}{dx}\right) $

  .container width:{45%}
    .box title:{Three things fall out at once} background:{.lightblue}
      1. **The law is linear in the gradient**, which is Darcy's observation, and it holds only while the tube flow is laminar.
      2. **$ K $ splits into two factors**: $ c\,d^2 $ belongs to the rock, $ \rho_w g/\mu $ belongs to the fluid. The next slide gives the rock part its own name.
      3. **$ K $ goes as the square of the pore diameter**, and pore diameter scales with grain diameter. Every empirical formula in the next section is a version of $ K \propto d^2 $.

    A factor of 10 in grain size is a factor of 100 in conductivity. That single line explains the whole range from gravel to clay.


# Where Darcy's law stops

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    **Too fast: turbulence.** The derivation assumed laminar tube flow. The test is a Reynolds number built on grain diameter,

    $ Re = \frac{\rho_w q d}{\mu} $

    and the law holds for $ Re $ below about 1 to 10.

    | Setting | $ q $ | $ d $ | $ Re $ |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Sand aquifer | 1 m/day | 1 mm | 0.01 |
    | Coarse gravel | 100 m/day | 10 mm | 12 |
    | Karst conduit, near a pumped well | much larger | much larger | $ \gg 10 $ |

    Beyond that, discharge grows more slowly than the gradient, and a drawdown predicted from Darcy's law comes out too small.

  .container width:{50%}
    **Too slow: a threshold.** In very low-permeability clays, water close to charged mineral surfaces is held tightly enough that a minimum gradient may be needed before anything moves at all (Bolt and Groenevelt, 1969).

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Both failures sit at the ends of the range. In between — which is nearly every aquifer, nearly always — Darcy's law is accurate enough to build the rest of the subject on, and we do exactly that in the theory lecture.

    Two places in this course where it fails and it matters: **karst conduits**, in the aquifers lecture, and the **immediate neighbourhood of a pumping well**, in the well hydraulics lecture.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Conductivity, permeability, and how to estimate them


# Hydraulic conductivity: thirteen orders of magnitude

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    !(88%)[Conductivity of aquifer materials](../image/franklin_fig_3-11.png "Hydraulic conductivity for selected aquifer materials, and their potential as an aquifer.")

  .container width:{55%}
    - $ K $ has units of length over time. Values run from $ 10^{-8} $ m/day in unfractured igneous rock to $ 10^{4} $ m/day in cavernous limestone.
    - Almost nothing else in the earth sciences varies over twelve or thirteen orders of magnitude. Porosity, by comparison, covers a factor of 50.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      **The consequence for how you spend money.** Getting the gradient wrong by a factor of two changes your answer by a factor of two. Getting the material wrong changes it by a factor of $ 10^{6} $. Site investigation therefore spends its budget on finding out what the ground is made of.

    - Read the figure with the fracturing in mind: for every rock type on it, the fractured version sits one to three decades to the right of the unfractured one.


# Intrinsic permeability

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    The derivation split $ K $ into a rock part and a fluid part. Name the rock part:

    $ q = -\frac{k\rho_w g}{\mu}\frac{dh}{dl} \qquad K = \frac{k\rho_w g}{\mu} $

    - $ k $ is the **intrinsic permeability**, with units of **area**: $ \mathrm{m^2} $, or $ \mathrm{cm^2} $, or darcy, where $ 1\ \mathrm{darcy} = 9.87\times10^{-13}\ \mathrm{m^2} $.
    - It is an area because it is the $ c\,d^2 $ of the derivation: a pore cross-section.
    - At 20 °C and 1 atm, with $ \rho_w = 998.2 $ kg/m³ and $ \mu = 1.002\times10^{-3} $ kg/(m s):

      $ K = 9.77\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{m^{-1}s^{-1}} \cdot k $

      $ k = 1.023\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{m\,s} \cdot K $

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Why anyone bothers} background:{.lightblue}
      $ K $ describes rock **and** water together. Change the fluid and $ K $ changes although the rock has not. Petroleum, contaminant and geothermal work all move fluids that are not water, so they quote $ k $.

    **A temperature effect you can check in the field.** Only $ \mu $ changes appreciably over the natural range:

    | | $ \mu $ (kg m⁻¹s⁻¹) | $ K $ relative |
    |---|---|---|
    | 5 °C | $ 1.52\times10^{-3} $ | 1.00 |
    | 25 °C | $ 0.89\times10^{-3} $ | 1.71 |

    The same rock conducts water **1.7 times faster** in a warm summer aquifer than in a cold winter one, with no change in the rock at all.


# Estimating K from grain size

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    The derivation gave $ K \propto d^2 $. The empirical formulas are that result with a fitted constant and a choice of which $ d $.

    | Source | Equation |
    |---|---|
    | Hazen (1911) | $ K = C d_{10}^2 $, C = 100–150 cm⁻¹s⁻¹ |
    | Harleman (1963) | $ k = 6.54\times10^{-4} d_{10}^2 $ |
    | Kozeny–Carman | $ K = \frac{\rho_w g}{\mu}\frac{n^3}{(1-n)^2}\frac{d_m^2}{180} $ |

    - $ d_{10} $ is the **effective grain size**: the sieve opening that 10% of the sample by weight passes through, so 90% of the grains are coarser.
    - Kozeny–Carman is the only one carrying porosity as well, through $ n^3/(1-n)^2 $, and it carries $ \rho_w g/\mu $ openly. It is the pore-bundle derivation with the geometry done properly.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Why the tenth percentile and not the mean?} background:{.lightred}
      Because the **fines** control the flow. A sand with 10% clay in it conducts like a clay, since the small grains sit in the throats between the large ones and it is the throats that resist. Using $ d_{10} $ builds that in.

      The same idea returns twice more today: the harmonic mean, and the vertical conductivity of a layered sequence. In each case the worst material sets the answer.

    Worked in the tutorial: $ d_{10} = 0.095 $ mm gives $ K \approx 6\times10^{-3} $ cm/s from Harleman and $ 9\times10^{-3} $ cm/s from Hazen. Agreement to a factor of 1.5 is as good as grain-size estimates get. Field tests remain the reliable method.


# Measuring K in the laboratory: constant head

.row
  .container width:{60%}
    Used for **coarse** material, $ K > 10^{-3} $ cm/s.

    - Hold the head difference $ h $ fixed across a core of length $ L $ and cross-section area $ A $.
    - Wait until the flow is steady, then measure the outflow rate $ Q $ by collecting water for a known time.
    - Darcy's law rearranged gives

      $ K = \frac{QL}{Ah} $

    - The overflow weir at the top is what holds the supply head constant. Without it the head falls as the reservoir drains and the test is no longer steady.
    - This is Darcy's own apparatus with the plumbing tidied up.

  .container width:{40%}
    !(58%)[Constant head](../image/franklin_fig_3-13-a.png "Constant-head permeameter: a fixed head difference Δh across a core of length ℓ.")


# Measuring K in the laboratory: falling head

.row
  .container width:{60%}
    Used for **fine** material: fine sands through to clays.

    - No steady supply. Water in a standpipe of cross-section area $ a $ drains through the core, and the head falls from $ h_0 $ to $ h_1 $ in time $ t_1 - t_0 $:

      $ K = 2.3\,\frac{aL}{A(t_1-t_0)}\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{h_0}{h_1}\right) $

    - The split between the two tests is practical. In a clay, $ Q $ in a constant-head test is too small to catch, and watching a level fall is easier than measuring a drip. The narrow standpipe is what makes the fall visible: a small $ a $ over a large $ A $ means a large drop in level for a small volume passed.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Both tests measure a core a few centimetres across, repacked or disturbed by the coring. Whether that number describes the aquifer is the subject of the next section.

  .container width:{40%}
    !(44%)[Falling head](../image/franklin_fig_3-13-b.png "Falling-head permeameter: the level in the standpipe falls from h₀ to h₁.")


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Real ground: heterogeneous and anisotropic


# Two independent questions about a piece of ground

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 340 250" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <text x="95" y="14" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">isotropic</text>
        <text x="255" y="14" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">anisotropic</text>
        <text x="8"  y="70" font-size="11" transform="rotate(-90 8,70)" text-anchor="middle">homogeneous</text>
        <text x="8"  y="190" font-size="11" transform="rotate(-90 8,190)" text-anchor="middle">heterogeneous</text>
        <rect x="25" y="25" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <circle cx="95" cy="70" r="26" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="185" y="25" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="45" x2="325" y2="45" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="70" x2="325" y2="70" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="95" x2="325" y2="95" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <ellipse cx="255" cy="70" rx="40" ry="16" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="25" y="145" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <circle cx="60" cy="175" r="16" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <circle cx="125" cy="205" r="26" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="185" y="145" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="165" x2="325" y2="171" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="195" x2="325" y2="201" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <ellipse cx="225" cy="180" rx="26" ry="10" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <ellipse cx="288" cy="212" rx="34" ry="13" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
      </svg>

    Each red outline shows how far water travels from a point in one unit of time.

  .container width:{55%}
    - **Homogeneous or heterogeneous** asks whether $ K $ changes from **place to place**.
    - **Isotropic or anisotropic** asks whether $ K $ changes with **direction** at one place.
    - The two are independent, and all four combinations occur. Real ground is the bottom right box.
    - A circle means the material treats every direction alike; an ellipse means it does not.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      The rest of this section makes one point: **heterogeneity at a small scale becomes anisotropy at a larger one.** A stack of layers, each of them isotropic, behaves as a single anisotropic material once you stop resolving the layers.


# K varies within a single unit

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Thirty-two measurements of $ K $, all from **one** geological unit:

    | | value (m/s) |
    |---|---|
    | smallest | $ 2\times10^{-10} $ |
    | largest | $ 1.5\times10^{-6} $ |
    | arithmetic mean $ \eta $ | $ 2.64\times10^{-7} $ |
    | geometric mean $ G $ | $ 9.93\times10^{-8} $ |
    | harmonic mean $ H $ | $ 5.72\times10^{-9} $ |

    - Four decades of spread inside one unit that a geologist would map as one colour.
    - The three means differ by a factor of **46**. Quoting "the" conductivity of a unit without saying which average you took is not a well-defined statement.

  .container width:{50%}
    - A histogram of $ K $ is strongly skewed; a histogram of $ \log K $ is close to normal. $ K $ is **log-normally distributed**, with $ \eta_{\log K} = -7.0 $ and $ \sigma = 0.76 $ in this sample.
    - That is why hydrogeologists quote geometric means and plot conductivity on log axes.

    .box title:{Which mean is the right one?} background:{.lightblue}
      It depends on how the flow meets the variation, and the next two slides derive exactly that:

      - flow **along** the variation → arithmetic mean
      - flow **across** it → harmonic mean
      - everything else lies between the two (Matheron, 1967), and for a statistically isotropic field the geometric mean is the usual estimate.


# Layers in parallel: the arithmetic mean

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Flow **along** the bedding. Each layer feels the same head drop over the same distance, so each has the same gradient $ i $, and the discharges add:

    $ Q = \sum_i K_{hi} b_i i \;=\; K_h \left(\sum_i b_i\right) i $

    $ K_h = \frac{\sum_i b_i K_{hi}}{\sum_i b_i} $

    a **thickness-weighted arithmetic mean**.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      The best layer dominates. One metre of gravel in a hundred metres of clay carries nearly all of the horizontal flow, and $ K_h $ barely notices the clay.

  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect x="40" y="25"  width="240" height="26" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="51"  width="240" height="16" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="67"  width="240" height="34" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="101" width="240" height="20" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="121" width="240" height="30" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <text x="292" y="42"  font-size="10">K₁ b₁</text>
        <text x="292" y="63"  font-size="10">K₂ b₂</text>
        <text x="292" y="88"  font-size="10">K₃ b₃</text>
        <line x1="55" y1="38"  x2="265" y2="38"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="59"  x2="120" y2="59"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="84"  x2="265" y2="84"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="111" x2="120" y2="111" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="136" x2="265" y2="136" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <text x="160" y="175" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">same gradient in every layer, discharges add</text>
        <defs><marker id="a3" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
      </svg>

    Each layer carries $ K_{hi} b_i i $. Thick, conductive layers carry almost all of it.


# Layers in series: the harmonic mean

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Flow **across** the bedding. Now every layer passes the same $ q $, and the head losses add:

    $ \Delta h = \sum_i q\,\frac{b_i}{K_{vi}} \;=\; \frac{q}{K_v}\sum_i b_i $

    $ K_v = \frac{\sum_i b_i}{\sum_i b_i/K_{vi}} $

    a **harmonic mean**.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      Now the worst layer dominates. One metre of clay in a hundred metres of gravel sets the vertical conductivity of the whole stack almost on its own, because the water has no way around it.

    This is the same asymmetry as resistors: in parallel the best path wins, in series the worst one does.

  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect x="40" y="25"  width="240" height="26" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="51"  width="240" height="16" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="67"  width="240" height="34" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="101" width="240" height="20" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="121" width="240" height="30" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="110" y1="20" x2="110" y2="158" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a4)"/>
        <line x1="210" y1="20" x2="210" y2="158" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a4)"/>
        <text x="292" y="63"  font-size="10" fill="#b43c3c">tight</text>
        <text x="292" y="115" font-size="10" fill="#b43c3c">tight</text>
        <text x="160" y="180" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">same q through every layer, head losses add</text>
        <defs><marker id="a4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
      </svg>

    The two grey layers occupy a fifth of the thickness and take nearly all of the head loss. A nest of piezometers through this stack would show head almost constant in the sandy layers and dropping steeply across the grey ones.


# Worked example: layering makes anisotropy

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    A 300 m sequence of interbedded sandstone and shale, 75% sandstone. **Both rocks are isotropic.**

    - Sandstone: $ K = 1\times10^{-5} $ m/s, 225 m in total
    - Shale: $ K = 1.92\times10^{-12} $ m/s, 75 m in total

    $ K_h = \frac{225(10^{-5}) + 75(1.92\times10^{-12})}{300} = 7.5\times10^{-6} $ m/s

    $ K_v = \frac{300}{\frac{225}{10^{-5}} + \frac{75}{1.92\times10^{-12}}} = 7.7\times10^{-12} $ m/s

    $ \frac{K_h}{K_v} \approx 10^{6} $

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Read the two answers} background:{.lightblue}
      $ K_h $ is three quarters of the sandstone value: the shale might as well not be there.

      $ K_v $ is almost exactly the shale value scaled by its share of the thickness: the sandstone might as well not be there.

    - Not one of the six numbers that went in was anisotropic. The factor of a million is produced **entirely by the arrangement**.
    - So "is this rock anisotropic?" is a question you cannot answer without saying at what scale you are looking.
    - This is why a regional groundwater model with 500 m cells uses $ K_h/K_v $ ratios of $ 10^2 $ to $ 10^4 $: the cell is bigger than the beds inside it.


# Anisotropy at two scales

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    ### Measured on a core

    | Material | Horizontal $ K $ (m/s) | Vertical $ K $ (m/s) | Ratio |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Anhydrite | $ 10^{-14} $–$ 10^{-12} $ | $ 10^{-15} $–$ 10^{-13} $ | 10 |
    | Chalk | $ 10^{-10} $–$ 10^{-8} $ | $ 5\times10^{-11} $–$ 5\times10^{-9} $ | 2 |
    | Limestone, dolomite | $ 10^{-9} $–$ 10^{-7} $ | $ 5\times10^{-10} $–$ 5\times10^{-8} $ | 2 |
    | Sandstone | $ 5\times10^{-13} $–$ 10^{-10} $ | $ 2.5\times10^{-13} $–$ 5\times10^{-11} $ | 2 |
    | Shale | $ 10^{-14} $–$ 10^{-12} $ | $ 10^{-15} $–$ 10^{-13} $ | 10 |
    | Salt | $ 10^{-14} $ | $ 10^{-14} $ | 1 |

  .container width:{45%}
    - A core is a piece of **one bed**. At that scale the anisotropy comes from the alignment of flat grains during deposition, and the ratio is about **2 to 10**.
    - The ratios of $ 10^3 $ to $ 10^6 $ that regional models use come from **layering**, by the calculation on the previous slide.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      Two different mechanisms, two different scales, the same word. Quote a ratio and say which one you mean, or the number is not interpretable.

    - The consequence is the same in both cases: water crosses a sedimentary sequence with great difficulty and travels along it easily. Which is what makes confined aquifers possible at all.


# Darcy's law when direction matters

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    In an anisotropic material $ q $ and the head gradient **point in different directions**, so the constant $ K $ becomes a tensor:

    $ \mathbf{q} = -\mathbf{K}\nabla h $

      $ \mathbf{K} = \begin{bmatrix} K_{xx} & K_{xy} & K_{xz} \\ K_{yx} & K_{yy} & K_{yz} \\ K_{zx} & K_{zy} & K_{zz} \end{bmatrix} $

    so that each component of flow responds to all three components of the gradient:

    $ q_x = -K_{xx}\frac{\partial h}{\partial x} - K_{xy}\frac{\partial h}{\partial y} - K_{xz}\frac{\partial h}{\partial z} $

    - The tensor is symmetric, $ K_{xy} = K_{yx} $, so six numbers describe it.
    - Choose the coordinate axes along the **principal directions of anisotropy** — for a layered sequence, along and across the bedding — and the off-diagonal terms vanish:

      $ q_x = -K_{xx}\frac{\partial h}{\partial x},\quad q_y = -K_{yy}\frac{\partial h}{\partial y},\quad q_z = -K_{zz}\frac{\partial h}{\partial z} $

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Anisotropy from layering](../image/internet_anisotropy.png "The same layered material in three orientations relative to the axes. Here σ is electrical conductivity, and hydraulic conductivity behaves in exactly the same way: the tensor components swap as the layering is reoriented, although the material is unchanged.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Consequence worth holding onto: in anisotropic ground, **flow lines do not cross equipotential lines at right angles**. That is an isotropic result. It is assumed on almost every map you will see.


# Flow lines bend at a boundary

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    Two conditions hold where flow crosses from unit 1 into unit 2, with $ \alpha $ measured from the **normal** to the boundary:

    - Mass is conserved, so the component of $ q $ **across** the boundary is continuous:

      $ q_1\cos\alpha_1 = q_2\cos\alpha_2 $

    - Head is continuous along the boundary, so the head gradient **along** it is the same on both sides. With $ q = -K\,dh/dl $ that means $ q_1\sin\alpha_1/K_1 = q_2\sin\alpha_2/K_2 $.

    Dividing one by the other gives the **tangent refraction law**:

    $ \frac{K_1}{K_2} = \frac{\tan\alpha_1}{\tan\alpha_2} $

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Refraction of flow lines](../image/franklin_fig_3-15.png "Flow line refraction at boundaries between materials of different permeability: in (a) k₂ > k₁, in (b) k₂ < k₁, and in panel c, k₂ ≫ k₁.")

    Take the limits and the law becomes easy to remember:

    - Into a **much tighter** unit: $ \alpha_2 \to 0 $, flow turns to cross it **straight down**.
    - Into a **much more conductive** unit: $ \alpha_2 \to 90° $, flow turns to run **along** it.


# What refraction means in the field

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    **Entering the shale.** Take the layered example again: water in the sandstone moving at 45° to the boundary enters the shale at

    $ \alpha_2 = \arctan\!\left(\frac{1.92\times10^{-12}}{10^{-5}}\tan 45°\right) \approx 0° $

    Whatever the flow was doing in the sandstone, it crosses the shale **vertically**. This is why cross sections show downward arrows through every confining bed, and why leakage through one can be treated as one-dimensional.

  .container width:{50%}
    **Entering the sand.** Reverse the contrast and the flow turns until it runs nearly parallel to the bedding. A thin permeable bed therefore gathers flow from the whole thickness above and below it, and carries it away laterally.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Those two results together are the reason aquifers and confining beds behave as they do. Water is collected sideways by the conductive units and passed vertically between them by the tight ones. Next week's lecture is about the units themselves.

    Both are visible in the cross section at the end of this lecture, where the flow arrows bend as they cross the contact.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Mapping a flow system


# Potentiometric surface maps

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - A **potentiometric surface** is the imaginary surface that everywhere matches the level to which water rises in wells completed in one aquifer (Meinzer, 1923). A map of it is contoured hydraulic head.
    - Three conditions have to hold for the map to mean anything.
      1. **One aquifer.** A deeper or shallower unit at the same place can have a completely different head, as the piezometer nest showed. You must know which unit each well is screened in.
      2. **Flow is horizontal in the aquifer.** Then equipotential lines are vertical, head does not change with depth, and it makes no difference where in the aquifer a well is screened or how long its screen is.
      3. **Contour spacing is the gradient.** The head drop between contours is fixed, so close contours mean a steep gradient and wide spacing means a flat one. This is how you read a map by eye.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Making a potentiometric map](../image/franklin_fig_3-18.png "Three steps: in (a) plot the wells with their heads; in (b) contour to get equipotential lines and add flow directions; in panel c, add flow lines to make a flow net.")

    Three steps: plot the measured heads, contour them, then add arrows at right angles to the contours pointing down-gradient.


# Two warnings about these maps

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{A potentiometric surface is not a water table} background:{.lightred}
      For an **unconfined** aquifer the two coincide, because the aquifer's top surface is where the water stands.

      For a **confined** aquifer the potentiometric surface lies **above** the top of the aquifer, often far above. It marks where water would rise if you drilled, and there is no water at that level.

      Where it lies above the ground, a well flows without pumping. That is an **artesian** bore, and it is how the Great Artesian Basin got its name.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Right angles are an assumption} background:{.lightred}
      Flow arrows are drawn at right angles to the contours. That is correct only in **isotropic** material.

      In layered ground, which is most ground, the flow direction is rotated away from the perpendicular, towards the direction of easier travel.

    - A third, practical warning: contouring requires the measurements to be **contemporaneous**. Heads move seasonally by metres, so a map assembled from readings taken across a year is contouring the calendar as much as the aquifer.


# Example: the Memphis aquifer

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    - The Memphis Sand, contoured from wells screened in that single unit, 1995.
    - Several well fields each produced a local **cone of depression**. The cones overlapped and merged into one regional low in the centre of the map.
    - Contours are closely spaced around the low, so the gradient there is steep, and every flow arrow points inward. The aquifer's natural discharge has been replaced by discharge to pumps.
    - Reduced pumping in later years has allowed water levels to recover, which is visible in the hydrograph on the next slide.

  .container width:{55%}
    !(84%)[Memphis potentiometric surface](../image/franklin_fig_3-19.png "Potentiometric surface of the Memphis aquifer in 1995, contour interval 10 ft, hachured where the contours enclose a depression. Red dots are the wells used as control.")


# Water-level hydrographs

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - A **water-level hydrograph** is head at one well against time. A map is a snapshot; a hydrograph is the history.
    - Historically, monthly visits with a tape. Now loggers reporting over satellite or mobile networks.
    - Well Sh:P-76 in the Memphis aquifer, monitored from 1925 to 1995, falls steadily for forty years and then levels off around 1970 as pumping is reduced.
    - What to read off one of these:
      - **Seasonal cycle**: recharge in the wet season, drawdown by irrigation in the dry.
      - **Trend**: the balance of long-term recharge against long-term abstraction.
      - **Steps**: a new well field, a new allocation rule, a drought.
    - Maps back in time are usually impossible to make because the measurements were never taken at enough wells at once. A long record at one well is what survives.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[A long hydrograph](../image/franklin_fig_3-20.png "Long-term hydrograph of well Sh:P-76, completed in the Memphis aquifer: water levels decline for about forty years and then stabilise.")


# Hydrogeological cross sections

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - A vertical slice through the flow system, drawn **along** the mean flow direction so that the picture is a fair two-dimensional representation.
    - It carries the stratigraphy, the conductivity of each unit, the head measurements from the piezometer nests along the line, and the equipotentials contoured from them.
    - In the example: a low-$ K $ unit over an aquifer. In the upper unit flow is nearly vertical with closely spaced equipotentials, so a steep gradient. In the aquifer the equipotentials are vertical and flow is horizontal with a gentle gradient. In the middle, the flow lines **refract** across the contact.
    - The vertical scale is exaggerated to make the layers visible. On an exaggerated section, flow lines and equipotentials genuinely do not meet at right angles, although they are usually drawn that way.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(88%)[Cross section](../image/franklin_fig_3-21.png "Head distribution in a two-layer system from four piezometer nests: downward flow through the upper low-conductivity unit, horizontal flow in the aquifer, refraction at the contact.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Everything in this lecture is in this figure: heads at points, a gradient, two conductivities, refraction, and a flow direction that follows from them.


