#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Weighing the groundwater from orbit


# Every method so far measures a point

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - A piezometer samples a volume metres across. A pumping test samples hundreds of metres. A basin is hundreds of kilometres across.
    - Worse, head is not water. Converting a change in head to a change in **volume** needs the storage properties of next week's lecture, and they are rarely known well.
    - Worse again, bores are not where the water is. They are where the drilling was paid for: near towns, near irrigation, near roads.
    - So the basin-scale question — **has this basin gained or lost water this decade** — cannot be answered by adding up bores.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Since 2002 it can be answered another way, by weighing the basin. Water has mass, mass has a gravity field, and a gravity field can be measured from orbit.

  .container width:{45%}
    .box title:{The size of the signal} background:{.lightblue}
      The Millennium Drought removed about **140 km³** of water from the Murray-Darling Basin.

      Spread over the basin's $ 1.06\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2} $ that is a layer

      $ \frac{140\ \mathrm{km^3}}{1.06\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2}} = 0.13\ \mathrm{m} $

      **Thirteen centimetres of water.** That is the thing being weighed from 490 km up.


# How GRACE weighs water

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 340 210" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <defs><marker id="a5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#333"/></marker></defs>
        <path d="M10,150 Q90,120 170,128 Q250,136 330,120 L330,200 L10,200 Z" fill="#c8e1c8" stroke="#6b8f6b"/>
        <ellipse cx="170" cy="155" rx="70" ry="20" fill="#add8e6" stroke="#1f6fb2"/>
        <text x="170" y="159" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">extra water mass</text>
        <line x1="120" y1="22" x2="215" y2="22" stroke="#333" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#a5)"/>
        <text x="167" y="16" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">direction of travel</text>
        <rect x="80" y="40" width="26" height="14" fill="#8a8a8a" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="215" y="40" width="26" height="14" fill="#8a8a8a" stroke="#333"/>
        <text x="93" y="70" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">trailing</text>
        <text x="228" y="70" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">leading</text>
        <line x1="106" y1="47" x2="215" y2="47" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4,3"/>
        <text x="160" y="43" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b43c3c">220 km, measured to µm</text>
        <line x1="93" y1="76" x2="93" y2="133" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <line x1="228" y1="76" x2="228" y2="133" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <text x="170" y="196" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">the leading satellite reaches the mass first and is pulled forward</text>
      </svg>

  .container width:{50%}
    - Two identical satellites, one chasing the other about 220 km behind, at roughly 490 km altitude.
    - A microwave link measures the **distance between them** continuously, to within a few micrometres.
    - Passing over a mass excess, the leading satellite is pulled forward and the separation grows; as the pair passes over, it shrinks again. The pattern of stretching and squeezing over a month is inverted into a global gravity field.
    - Everything that is not water is modelled and removed: atmospheric mass, ocean tides, and the slow rebound of the crust from the last ice age. Over Australia that last correction is small, which makes the continent an unusually clean target.
    - What remains is reported as **equivalent water thickness**, in millimetres of water, as an anomaly against a reference mean.


# What the measurement can and cannot resolve

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - A monthly gravity field is expressed in spherical harmonics to about degree 50, which corresponds to features of about **400 km** on the ground. Newer mascon solutions are reported on finer grids, and the true resolution is still set by the physics, not the grid spacing.
    - So GRACE sees **basins**, not fields, not bores. Published studies use areas of at least $ 10^{5} $ to $ 10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2} $.
    - The Murray-Darling, at $ 1.06\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2} $, is right at the smallest size that can be resolved with confidence. Studying the Murrumbidgee alone this way is not possible.
    - Signal from outside the basin bleeds in and signal from inside bleeds out. This is **leakage**, and correcting for it is most of the work in a GRACE study.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Three more limitations to state plainly} background:{.lightred}
      - **No depth information.** Gravity gives a mass change in a column. It cannot say whether the water left a reservoir, the soil or an aquifer at 200 m.
      - **Monthly, at best.** No storm, no single recharge event.
      - **A gap in the record.** GRACE ran 2002 to 2017, GRACE-FO began in 2018, and the eleven months between them are missing from every time series.

    In exchange you get the one thing no ground network provides: a complete, unbiased total for the whole basin, every month, for twenty years.


# From total storage to groundwater

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Gravity measures **all** the water in the column at once:

    $ \Delta \mathrm{TWS} = \Delta S_{snow} + \Delta S_{surface} + \Delta S_{soil} + \Delta S_{groundwater} $

    Groundwater is obtained by subtracting the others, each from an independent source:

    $ \Delta S_{gw} = \Delta \mathrm{TWS} - \Delta S_{surface} - \Delta S_{soil} $

    - Surface water from reservoir and river records; soil moisture from a land surface model or from satellite soil moisture; snow is negligible in the Murray-Darling.
    - Every error in those terms lands in the groundwater estimate, which is why the method is only as good as its soil moisture.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Does the subtraction actually work?} background:{.lightblue}
      Leblanc et al. (2009) tested it over the Murray-Darling against ground measurements.

      - Annual GRACE storage against the sum of measured groundwater, surface water and soil moisture: **R = 0.94**.
      - GRACE minus soil moisture and surface water, against measured groundwater alone: **R = 0.92**.

      The residual really does track the bores.

    **This is what Assignment II and Assignment III ask you to do**, with CSR mascons for storage and the ESA CCI record for soil moisture.


# The Millennium Drought, weighed

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Murray-Darling Basin, 2001 to 2007, from Leblanc et al. (2009):

    | Store | Change | Over what period |
    |---|---|---|
    | Soil moisture | $ -80\ \mathrm{km^3} $ | 2001 to 2003 |
    | Surface water | $ -12\ \mathrm{km^3} $ | 2001 to 2003 |
    | Groundwater | $ -104\ \mathrm{km^3} $ | 2001 to 2007 |

    - Average total deficit over the GRACE record: **140 km³**. Peak deficit, January to March 2007: **240 km³**.
    - Soil moisture and surface water emptied within **two years** and then had nothing left to give. Groundwater was still falling **six years** in.
    - Rainfall returned to average in 2007. Groundwater storage did not.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{What the sequence tells you} background:{.lightblue}
      A drought moves through the stores in order of their residence times. Rainfall deficit first, then soil moisture in months, then streamflow, then groundwater over years, and groundwater recovers last of all.

      A rainfall record says the drought ended in 2007. The aquifer says it had not.

    - This is the argument for measuring storage instead of inferring it from rainfall, and it is why this lecture ends on a gravity mission.
    - The physics of *why* groundwater empties slowly is everything earlier in this lecture: low $ K $, long paths, and residence times of decades.


# What we learned

.row
  .container width:{100%}
    - **Storage and transmission are different properties.** Porosity says how much water is held; conductivity says how fast it moves. Clay is the extreme case: highest porosity, lowest conductivity.
    - **Head, not pressure, sets the direction of flow.** $ h = z + P/\rho_w g $, and in still water it is constant while pressure is not. Head belongs to a point, so one site can have several heads.
    - **Darcy's law**, $ q = -K\,dh/dl $, is a measured relationship with a pore-scale explanation: $ K \propto d^2 \rho_w g/\mu $. That single result explains the range from gravel to clay, the empirical grain-size formulas, and the split into permeability and fluid properties.
    - **Divide by effective porosity** to get the speed the water actually travels. In fractured rock that factor is a hundred or more.
    - **Layering turns heterogeneity into anisotropy.** Along the bedding, the arithmetic mean and the best layer wins; across it, the harmonic mean and the worst layer wins. Isotropic layers alone can give $ K_h/K_v = 10^6 $.
    - **Flow lines refract** at a contact: straight down into a tight unit, along a conductive one.
    - **Field practice**: piezometer nests for vertical gradients, potentiometric maps for one aquifer at one time, hydrographs for the history, and GRACE for the whole basin.

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    .container width:{60%} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.2cm} background:{.lightred} radius:{0.5cm}
      Next week: **aquifers**. The units themselves, and how much water they release when the head falls.
